motivation:
I ran `make timing TARGET=bench` on the champion r4s1 (place option 0,
92.7 MHz; median 99.6). The top 25 paths are three families, all
within 0.45 ns of each other (data delay 10.32-10.75 ns). Fixing only
one of them buys nothing.

(A) ALU result, paths 1/3/5-9. Example path:
  - MEM/WB.wb_data_7;
  - alu_a_7_s1 -> alu_a_7_s0 (2-LUT operand AND-OR);
  - diff carry chain, SUM out at 11.30 ns;
  - n1384_s14 -> s13 -> s11 -> s6 -> s2 -> s0: SIX single-fanout LUTs
    in a LINEAR chain;
  - EX/MEM.alu_result_20, at 16.08 ns.
Gowin synthesis turned the 8-leg result AND-OR plus the is_muldiv ?
m_result mux into a chain. It fed the latest-arriving leg (sum/diff,
at the end of the carry chain) into the FIRST LUT of that chain. So
4.8 ns of the 10.75 ns path is spent after the adder. A balanced
select, with the adder leg entering the last LUT, needs 1-2 levels.

(B) Redirect, path 2 and others:
  - ID/EX.b_x (fanout 32, 2.93 ns route);
  - 2-LUT alu_b;
  - compare chain;
  - branch_taken_s3 -> branch_taken_s1 (branch_op mux);
  - redirect_fire_s1 -> redirect_fire_s0;
  - redirect_q.
That is 4 LUTs after the compare, although only the compare result
is late. fire_u, fire_t, fire_n and branch_op are all early.

(C) IF predict, paths 4/10/13/15/16/20/22/23/25:
  - lfsr_2 (the fetch word);
  - u_id/b_four_s2 -> b_four_s3. Synthesis merged if_stage's is_jal
    decode with id_stage's b_four (op_jal|op_jalr) into one net with
    fanout 27 and a 3.31 ns route;
  - is_jal ? imm_j : imm_b mux;
  - pred_target carry chain, to bit [20];
  - pred_taken (2 LUTs, fanout 33);
  - n419_s1 -> n419_s0 (next_pc / redirect / stall mux);
  - pc.

Each family is a late signal pushed through several LUTs or a
shared, badly placed high-fanout net. Early-arriving control is
never pre-combined. All the fixes below are pure restructurings: no
cycle changes and no new state except duplicated flops.

