motivation:
The champion (r4s1, 224.29, median 99.6 MHz, 4.44M cycles) has one
worst-path family left: the EX forwarding loop.
  from cpu/u_mem/reg_q.wb_data_7 (the MEM/WB forward source fw)
  to   cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_20
  23 cells, 12 logic levels, modules u_ex -> u_ex/u_alu -> u_ex.
The chain is:
  - fw / fx flop;
  - the one-hot operand AND-OR (4-5 legs = 2 LUT levels, fanout ~40);
  - the ALU leg (shifter or adder carry chain);
  - the one-hot result AND-OR (2 levels);
  - the is_muldiv ? m_result : alu_result mux (1 level);
  - EX/MEM.alu_result.
The redirect is now registered, so this loop sets Fmax on its own. The
same 2-level operand AND-OR also sits at the head of three other EX
paths:
  - the branch compare -> redirect_q;
  - the AGU -> dmem address register;
  - the muldiv operand latch.
The operand AND-OR only exists because forwarding is resolved at the
start of EX, from the EX/MEM and MEM/WB registers. Every select is
already computed one cycle early in ID. The only thing still late is
the data. So the mux can move to the other side of the clock edge.
Lessons r3s1 and r4s1 show that each LUT level removed from this loop
paid about 5% Fmax.

